Date: Mon, 14 Jun 93 21:49:36 PDT
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: SOC: Birth Order and Extropians

Scott DeLancey writes:

>       Face it--anyone who takes an interest in the stuff that
> goes on on this list, but claims to be less likely than most to
> buy a new theory, is living in a pretty strange reality (to put
> it diplomatically).

Well, I guess it's settled, then. :-}

As apparently only one of 2 who were first-born and who felt
themselves less likely to buy a new theory....

As there was nothing in Robin's poll to define what was meant by "buy
a new theory" (or whatever wording he used...I'm just using Scott's),
I interepreted it liberally.

Being a hard-headed rationalist, I find myself much more skeptical and
analytical about new theories than others. I find this to be fully
consistent with my first-born status, having to deal with the cold
harsh world on my own. My younger brother (12 years younger) is much
more "nurturing" (a blecherous term, but it applies), more yielding,
more gullible, less skeptical, and much more willing to accept a new
theory. (About my younger sister, I'll say nothing, except to note she
is very credulous about all new things she hears: "How do you know it
can't happen, Tim?")

I view 99% of all theories I hear as being bogus, and that all I have
to do is to look hard enough and I'll find the bogosities. What I
might "want" to believe is beside the point.

This approach has served me well and is probably why the majority of
scientists are first-born. Accepting new theories is only a small
fraction of the work done...most of science lies in rejecting theories
that fail in one or more places.

The "new ideas" on this list are not really all that wild, at least
not in the spectrum of wild ideas that most people routinely believe.
The science of nanotech, gene-splicing, whatever, is fairly solid
(even if I don't see the Singularity coming any time soon, for a
variety of reasons I've written about many times). Cryonics is iffier,
but I agree with the Pascal's wager line of reasoning...and it sure
beats the alternative. And so on.

And the heated arguments on this list show that many of us, at least
the active posters (who I would bet are 80% first borns!), are in fact
deeply suspicious and skeptical of new ideas. We want to see
persuasive evidence, proof.

Hardly surprising.


-Tim


