Date: Tue, 15 Jun 1993 01:52:35 -0400 (EDT)
From: esr@snark.thyrsus.com (Eric S. Raymond)
Subject: PHIL: Women, Fire, and Dangerous Things

Looking back over my recent postings, I see that I've recommended George
Lakoff's book "Women, Fire, and Dangerous Things" twice now in specific
contexts having to with the objectivism/subjectivism dispute and color
discrimination in human languages.

Let me make this general.  If you have any interest in philosophy,
psycholinguistics, AI, or cognitive psychology, *you must read this book*.
If you are either (a) an Objectivist, or (b) frequently argue with
Objectivists, *you must read this book*.  If anything in the General Semantics
thread made you sit up and think, *you must read this book*.

The title of WF&DT comes from Lakoff's discussion of an Australian language
called Dyirbal, a classifier language in which all nouns fall into one
of four categories: 1) Men and animals, 2) Women, fire and dangerous things,
3) Edible plants, and 4) Everything else.  Most generally, the topic of the
book is how language categories are formed and defined, and how they
shape the thinking of language users.

Lakoff calls himself a "cognitive linguist", and the book is a profound
criticism of several fundamental methodological assumptions in the field
(he does a particularly nice job of demolishing the Chomskian orthodoxy
of natural-language grammar as the product of an abstract-syntax machine).

But Lakoff is after bigger game than just linguistics in this book.  He
traces the failure of linguistics at grappling with pragmatic issues in
language use to a *philosophical* failure --- the inability of Aristotelian
metaphysics to describe the categorization processes actually used in
language cognition.

And that's just where the fun *starts*.  Read this --- you won't regret it!
-- 
							>>eric>>

